  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 AM EST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nThis morning's observations show Upper Michigan coming under the \ninfluence of chilly high pressure after yesterday's LES event. \nNighttime Microphysics shows mid-level banded cloud structures \nstreaming onshore from Lake Superior into the northwest snow belts. \nMeanwhile, radar returns confirm the winding-down of snowfall, with \nreturns indicating diminishing flurries in the eastern UP over the \npast few hours. Winds have backed to westerly and are calm to light \nover land, with the exception of areas near lakeshores, which are \nseeing gusts over 20 mph. RAP surface analysis shows high pressure \ncentered over the Northern Plains at 1035 mb, with ridging to 1029 \nmb extending over the UP. Last, temperatures are finally feeling \nlike winter - the eastern UP is mostly in the mid 20s while interior \nlocations have dropped as low as 13F. This is well on track to \nachieve minimums 0-10F below normal for tonight.\n\nOver the next few days, anomalously high ridging (500 mb heights at \n98th percentile of climatology, per the LREF) moves east and \nstrengthens. With continued westerly CAA under ridging, temperatures \narrive at seasonal for highs and slightly below seasonal (0-10F, \nspecifically) for lows through Wednesday night. A warming trend \ntakes place through the remainder of the week, with highs rising \nfrom the low 30s today to the low 40s by Friday. Meanwhile, \nconditions remain dry through Thanksgiving Day as a dry air advects \ninto the region. Cloud cover lingers for today and tomorrow as the \nlow-levels hold onto a shallow saturated layer as the center of the \nhigh transits south of us before clearing somewhat late Wednesday \ninto Thursday.\n\nFor later in the week, nonzero chances of precipitation enter the \nforecast on Friday as a deepening low swings north from the \nTennessee Valley toward New England. These remain low (<30%) and \nbrush only southern Menominee County and eastern Schoolcraft and \nLuce Counties. Ensembles are showing a mix of frozen and unfrozen p-\ntypes, which is valid considering that this low originates from the \ndeparting trough which just instigated the recent LES event. This \npiece of energy has marginal 850mb temps initially (in the -4 to -\n6C) range, so p-type becomes a question of how much this airmass \nwill be modified over the 3-4 days as it transits south from the \nNorthern Plains to the southeast, and back north to New England. \nPatchy precipitation chances between 10% and 40% linger through the \nweekend (higher on Sunday), as a weak Alberta Clipper follows this \nfirst low. Again, p-type is uncertain at this range given marginal \ntemperature profiles. Probabilistically, the NBM estimates rain will \nbe dominant for most of Saturday (very roughly 30% rain vs. 10%\nsnow), and snow will be dominant for Sunday (40% snow vs. 5% \nrain). In terms of QPF, NBM probabilities of greater than 0.10\" \n(liquid) inches range from 20-40% for Saturday, and 20-60% for \nSunday (greatest closer to Lake Superior). Check future forecast\npackages for emerging details for post-Thanksgiving travel.",
